        "title": "Demystifying your dream world",
        "text": "A  As a teenager, Brenda Giguere went ice skating with her friends every week, but they improved much faster than she did. She could only go round in circles, and she got tired of watching her friends switch easily from skating backwards to skating forwards. Lying in bed one night, she decided to try to practise those backward moves in her sleep. 'Before long I was dreaming I was skating, and I got very excited. It was so realistic. I felt the sensation of skating backwards — the movement of my legs, the cool air, the feeling of pushing myself along. Suddenly it all made sense as a set of logical, fluid, sequential body movements.'\n\nB  Brenda later found out that she had had what is called a 'lucid dream': a dream in which you are aware that you are dreaming. Lucid dreaming is one of the most controversial areas of dream research, partly because of misunderstandings about how much people can influence their dreams — or indeed, whether they should. Those in favour say that lucidity is an important step in understanding dreams, and argue that lucid dreams can take the horror out of nightmares, inspire new ideas, help the body to heal itself and solve mysteries of the mind that can improve a person's well-being.\n\nC  Lucid dreaming is a technique that has been practised by Tibetan Buddhist priests for more than a thousand years. The writings of the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle also refer to the conscious exploration of dreams. And when the discovery of rapid eye movement (REM) sleep years ago opened up new areas of sleep research, it also strengthened the argument for lucidity. Today, the leading expert in this field is Stephen LaBerge, who founded the Lucidity Institute in 1987.\n\nD  LaBerge believes that the state of awareness reached during lucid dreaming is similar to that of being awake. With colleagues, he has developed electronic devices that remind the dreamer during REM sleep to become lucid. The 'NovaDreamer' is a sleep mask that gives out a flashing light or sound signals when the user is dreaming (which it detects from eye movement). LaBerge claims that this makes the dreamer three times more likely to become lucid, as shown by research he has carried out. Ed Wirth, who has used the NovaDreamer, says the flashing light becomes part of his dreams, like the flickering picture on a TV screen. Of the 600 or so dreams a year that Wirth remembers, only five or six are lucid, but their effect is powerful and overwhelming. He dies in his dreams, and walks through walls: 'You can turn a threatening situation into a funny one. It removes the whole nightmare. Those dreams have actually changed my life. For me, it's an exploration.'\n\nE  Not everyone shares this enthusiasm. Rosalind Cartwright, the grande dame of sleep medicine research, believes the whole idea has been exaggerated. Cartwright, director of the Sleep Disorders Service and Research Center at Rush University Medical Center in Chicago, says: 'It's a wish to control things outside their usual function and time. It is trying to redesign the mind in a way I don't think is necessarily helpful. It gives people false hope.'\n\nF  LaBerge admits he does not have all the answers yet, but he feels lucky to be able to work in such a fascinating field. His goals are simple: to learn more about lucid dreaming and to make it more accessible to the public. At the moment, he is experimenting with chemicals that increase the release of acetylcholine, the main neurotransmitter in REM sleep, to help experienced lucid dreamers to have more lucid dreams. He is also testing herbal supplements such as galantamine, which comes from daffodil bulbs, to produce a similar effect. But LaBerge regrets that not enough research is being done. Research money is not exactly pouring in for lucid dreaming, and his business runs on a very small budget, with a staff of six, lots of volunteers, and funding from grants, donations and sales."
